    "1.0.0-rc1": "Major quality and security improvements: Implemented comprehensive multi-layered static code analysis system with 7 GitLab CI jobs (Bandit, Semgrep, Pylint, MyPy, code complexity), added 18+ pre-commit hooks for immediate feedback, implemented legacy algorithm warning system for deprecated cryptographic algorithms, applied comprehensive code formatting and consistency improvements via Black and isort, enhanced CI pipeline with Docker improvements and better job isolation, and cleaned up repository by removing unnecessary development artifacts. Prepared for 1.0.0 release with industry-leading code quality standards.",
    "1.0.0-rc2": "Critical production-readiness fixes: Resolved critical MyPy type errors that could cause runtime failures in PQC cipher operations, fixed variable naming conflicts between AESGCM and PQCipher classes, corrected string/bytes type assignments in password handling, removed invalid function parameters causing TypeErrors, fixed pytest test function return warnings, and corrected import paths for proper module resolution. Updated NEXT.md documentation to reflect that HQC algorithm support (hqc-128/192/256-hybrid) is fully implemented and production-ready. Security analysis confirmed 0 HIGH/MEDIUM severity issues. Reduced critical type errors from 529 to ~480 (90%+ critical runtime issues resolved). All core encryption functionality verified working. Ready for stable 1.0.0 production release.",
    "1.0.0-rc3": "Major documentation consolidation and enhancement release: Consolidated 37+ scattered documentation files into 10 comprehensive guides (73% reduction) improving organization, searchability, and user experience. Updated README.md with clickable navigation links. Enhanced CHANGELOG.md with complete version history from v0.1.0 to v1.0.0-rc3. Comprehensive RELEASE_NOTES.md overhaul with complete project narrative. Integrated ML-KEM CLI support documentation into algorithm-reference.md. Integrated HQC algorithm completion status into consolidated documentation. Enhanced user-guide.md with comprehensive manual installation steps for liboqs-python supporting multiple platforms. Improved documentation structure with logical categorization (User, Security, Technical, Project docs), comprehensive table of contents, cross-referencing, and consistent formatting. All technical content preserved while dramatically improving accessibility and maintainability.",
    "1.0.0": "STABLE RELEASE: Production-ready secure file encryption with comprehensive post-quantum cryptography support. Features ML-KEM (512/768/1024), HQC (128/192/256), and Kyber hybrid encryption algorithms alongside 18 classical encryption methods. Complete security suite with 7 hash functions, 3 key derivation methods, secure memory handling, and constant-time operations. Industry-leading code quality with comprehensive static analysis, 18+ pre-commit hooks, and extensive test coverage. Enhanced documentation with consolidated guides and comprehensive API reference. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Ready for production deployment in security-critical environments.",
    "1.0.1": "UI/UX ENHANCEMENT: Implemented segregated CLI help system with two-tier structure (global overview + command-specific options). Enhanced user experience with context-aware help display showing only relevant options per command. Improved command discovery with comprehensive overview in global help. Added version-aware algorithm filtering to maintain 1.0.x series compatibility. All existing functionality and file formats unchanged. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Focused release improving usability without modifying core cryptographic capabilities.",
    "1.0.2": "SECURITY FIX & MAINTENANCE: Removed AES-OCB3 algorithm due to security concerns with short nonces and implementation issues affecting key length validation. Updated CLI to block AES-OCB3 for new encryptions while preserving decryption of existing files. Changed default quick preset from AES-OCB3 to AES-GCM. Other algorithm deprecations postponed to version 1.2.0 for broader compatibility. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Focused security release maintaining backward compatibility for all other algorithms.",
    "1.0.3": "DOCUMENTATION & MAINTENANCE: Updated README documentation and version bump for PyPI deployment. Minor maintenance release focusing on documentation improvements and package distribution updates. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development.",
    "1.1.0": "MAJOR CRYPTOGRAPHIC ENHANCEMENT: Extended hash algorithm support with complete SHA-2 family (SHA-224/384) and SHA-3 family (SHA3-224/384), added BLAKE3 ultra-fast hashing and SHAKE-128 extendable-output function. Implemented RFC 5869 HKDF key derivation with configurable hash algorithms and iteration rounds. Added comprehensive post-quantum signature support with MAYO-1/3/5 multivariate and CROSS-128/192/256 code-based algorithms. Complete GUI redesign with professional hash family organization (SHA-2, SHA-3, BLAKE, SHAKE, Legacy) and dedicated KDF Algorithm Settings section. Enhanced CLI with full coverage of all hash algorithms and HKDF configuration options. Improved security posture by reclassifying PBKDF2 as legacy KDF (disabled by default). Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Extensive testing infrastructure with 93+ CLI parameter validation, dual implementation consistency testing, removal of deprecated pqc-allow-mixed-operations parameter, and systematic cleanup of 63 development artifacts. Comprehensive documentation updates reflecting modern vs. legacy algorithm categorization.",
    "1.2.0": "PROFESSIONAL FLUTTER DESKTOP & SECURITY HARDENING: Complete migration from tkinter to Flutter desktop providing native Wayland and X11 support, advanced CLI integration with real-time progress monitoring, comprehensive settings system with theme switching and cryptographic defaults. Desktop UX excellence with menu bar, keyboard shortcuts, drag & drop file operations, NavigationRail sidebar, tabbed interface, and algorithm recommendation engine with security guidance. Added advanced algorithm configuration UI for all KDFs (Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon, HKDF) and post-quantum algorithms (ML-KEM, Kyber, HQC, MAYO, CROSS). Enhanced security through cryptographic hardening: permanently removed deprecated PBKDF2 key derivation and Whirlpool hash algorithms from new encryption operations (existing encrypted files with these algorithms can still be decrypted). Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Streamlined Flatpak architecture removing complex X11/XWayland compatibility layers. Professional desktop integration with native dialogs and comprehensive JSON schema validation system for keystore and metadata structures.",
    "1.2.1": "Documentation maintenance release: Minor updates to documentation, user guides, and help text to improve clarity and user experience. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. No functional changes to cryptographic operations or core features.",
    "1.3.0": "CRYPTOGRAPHIC ENHANCEMENT & BUG FIXES: Added RandomX proof-of-work key derivation function optimized for CPUs with light (256MB) and fast (2GB) modes. Fixed critical default configuration decryption failure caused by metadata generation inconsistency between PBKDF2 parameter defaults and security template settings. Resolved post-quantum cryptography dual encryption test failures through improved test data parsing with binary prefix handling. Enhanced Docker build optimization reducing image size to 140MB with proper runtime library dependencies. Implemented comprehensive debug logging system replacing print statements. Added security enhancements: automatic application of default hash/KDF configuration when none specified to prevent direct password encryption, and security warnings when KDFs would operate directly on user password without sufficient entropy from prior hashing. Maintained full backward compatibility for all existing encrypted files while improving new encryption consistency. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development.",
    "1.3.1": "SECURITY & MAINTENANCE: Critical HSM security fix removing --device=all from Flatpak manifest, requiring explicit USB device opt-in for hardware security modules. Fixed post-quantum private key storage for AEAD algorithms. Enhanced Flatpak permissions with explicit Yubikey/smart card device access. Cleaned up Flatpak manifest removing invalid filesystem permissions.",
    "1.3.2": "SECURITY & PERFORMANCE MONITORING: Implemented comprehensive decryption time/memory estimation system to prevent DoS attacks via inflated metadata parameters. Added static benchmark data for all hash algorithms (SHA-224/256/384/512, SHA3-224/256/384/512, BLAKE2b/3, SHAKE-128/256) and KDF operations (PBKDF2, Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon, HKDF, RandomX). Pre-decryption cost display shows operation breakdown with conservative estimates, warns when thresholds exceeded (>10 seconds or >1GB memory), and provides 2-second cancellation window. Added --no-estimate CLI flag to suppress display for trusted files. Fixed critical argument parsing bug where --no-estimate failed when used with --progress flag due to preprocessor moving global flags before subcommand detection. Enhanced CLI robustness with improved subcommand detection after global flag preprocessing. Benchmarking system uses empirically validated non-linear scaling for memory-hard KDFs (Balloon: space_cost^0.85, Scrypt: exponential doubling). All existing encrypted files maintain full backward compatibility. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development.",
    "1.3.3": "LICENSE COMPLIANCE: Updated license declaration from MIT to Hippocratic License 3.0 across all distribution channels (PyPI, Flatpak). Fixed Flatpak metainfo.xml project_license field and added explicit license field to setup.py. No functional changes to cryptographic operations or features. Repository-wide license standardization for proper compliance.",
    "1.3.4": "CRITICAL SECURITY FIX (CVSSv3 8.1 HIGH): Fixed predictable salt derivation vulnerability (CWE-330) in multi-round KDF operations. Implemented Format Version 7 with secure chained salt derivation where each round uses previous round output as salt, preventing precomputation attacks. Affects all multi-round operations: BLAKE2b, BLAKE3, SHAKE-256, Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon, PBKDF2, HKDF. Full backward compatibility for decrypting Format Versions 3-6. Files encrypted with v1.3.4+ require v1.3.4 or newer to decrypt.",
    "1.3.5": "BLAKE3 HASH INTEGRATION: Added BLAKE3 keyed hashing support with 32-byte keys for format_version 7. Implemented BLAKE3-aware buffer sizing (64 bytes minimum) for proper key derivation with zero-initialization for deterministic hashing. Enhanced BLAKE3 detection in both flat (v3) and nested (v4+) hash config formats. Added comprehensive debug logging for BLAKE3 operations. Included build scripts for liboqs dependencies and Flatpak CI jobs. Fully backward compatible with v1.3.0-1.3.4 encrypted files and cross-version compatible with v1.4.x.",
    "1.4.0": "POST-QUANTUM ASYMMETRIC ENCRYPTION: Major release introducing Format Version 7 with post-quantum asymmetric encryption capabilities. Added ML-KEM-768 (Kyber) for key encapsulation and ML-DSA-65 (Dilithium) for digital signatures, providing quantum-resistant security. Implemented comprehensive Identity Management System with dual keypairs (encryption + signing), private key encryption at rest (Argon2id + AES-256-GCM), and identity storage in ~/.openssl_encrypt/identities/. Added multiple recipients support allowing single file encryption for N recipients with minimal overhead (~1.2KB per recipient). Implemented DoS Protection via signature verification before expensive KDF operations (fast ~1-5ms signature check prevents KDF attacks). Added complete Identity CLI with 7 commands (create, list, show, export, import, delete, change-password) for identity lifecycle management. Enhanced asymmetric encryption CLI with --for (multiple recipients), --sign-with (sender identity), --key (recipient for decryption), --verify-from (signature verification), and --no-verify flags. Integrated HSM Plugin Support with YubiKey Challenge-Response and plugin system architecture. Added benchmarking system for performance analysis and decryption time estimator for progress feedback. Comprehensive security enhancements: secure memory management (CryptoKey class, SecureBytes, secure_memzero), metadata integrity via digital signatures over canonical metadata, defense in depth with KDF chain running even in asymmetric mode. New modules: pqc_signing.py (450 lines), identity.py (750 lines), asymmetric_core.py (650 lines), identity_cli.py (540 lines). Extended modules: crypt_core.py (+700 lines), pqc.py (+100 lines). Added 120 new tests covering all asymmetric features with zero warnings. Format V7 maintains full backward compatibility with V6 symmetric encryption. No new dependencies (uses existing cryptography, argon2-cffi, liboqs-python). Production-ready with comprehensive documentation (ASYMMETRIC_ENCRYPTION_GUIDE.md), full test coverage, and security audit.",
    "1.4.0b8": "CRITICAL SECURITY FIX (BETA): Implemented Format Version 9 with secure chained salt derivation, resolving predictable salt issue in v1.4.x branch. Unified security model where v7 and v9 use secure chained derivation (unpredictable), v8 uses predictable derivation (backward compatibility only). Fixed critical pepper plugin scoping errors causing 100+ test failures. Added comprehensive Flatpak CI/CD pipeline with build and publish stages. Enhanced Flutter GUI with remote pepper plugin integration, integrity verification, cascade encryption UI, asymmetric encryption configuration, and YubiKey touch prompt improvements. Fixed HSM plugin loading and dependency management. Beta release addressing security vulnerability discovered in earlier v1.4.0 betas.",
    "1.4.0b9": "STABILITY & COMPATIBILITY (BETA): Test infrastructure improvements and cross-version compatibility fixes. Fixed 6 salt derivation test failures by correcting test suite to use generate_key() instead of multi_hash_password() for KDF operations. Completed Threefish-512/1024 cipher support with HKDF key expansion and proper AEAD integration. Fixed BLAKE3 buffer sizing for backward compatibility with pre-BLAKE3 encrypted files. Fixed critical metadata v7 schema compatibility with v1.3.4 (made 'mode' field optional). Resolved Scrypt bytearray conversion issues and XChaCha20 SecureBytes slice handling. Enhanced debug logging for version-aware salt derivation decisions. Full test suite: 1535 tests passing, 0 failures. Format Version 9 security model validated across all KDF algorithms (PBKDF2, Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon).",
    "1.4.0b10": "INDEPENDENT XOR & PARALLEL PROCESSING (BETA): Implemented Format Version 11 with Massey's Independent XOR composition providing 'strongest component' security guarantee where key remains secure even if all algorithms except the strongest are broken. Added optional parallel KDF processing with ~2.7x performance improvement (8.5s → 3.1s with 8 algorithms on 8-core CPU) using ProcessPoolExecutor for true CPU parallelism. New CLI flags: --independent-xor enables v11 format, --parallel-kdf enables parallel processing (requires --independent-xor), --kdf-workers N specifies worker count (default: auto-detect). Added comprehensive parallel_kdf.py module (739 lines) with Queue-based progress aggregation and GUI integration. Enhanced decrypt path with format version 11 support in 5 key locations. Added metadata_v11_schema.json with xor_mode field to distinguish Independent XOR (v11) from Sequential XOR (v10). Includes 7 new v11 Independent XOR tests and 11 parallel KDF tests. Full test suite: 1573 tests passing (up from 1535). Cross-branch compatible with v9 format in 1.3.x branch for seamless migration.",
    "1.4.0rc1": "FIRST RELEASE CANDIDATE: Comprehensive security hardening addressing 14 Dependabot security advisories. Fixes include CSPRNG for steganography (GHSA-vfgx-5q85-58q3), HKDF salt parameter (GHSA-j9mh-57cc-665x), standard PBKDF2 fallback (GHSA-743f-89fg-x288), sandbox bypass prevention (GHSA-mcjj-qw7m-j3cp), import guard synchronization (GHSA-9pgj-v69p-q586), path traversal sanitization (GHSA-8jpj-w975-rwv5), thread-safe module hiding (GHSA-43r4-3hf9-m84q), CORS default hardening (GHSA-c65f-x25w-62jv), trusted proxy restriction (GHSA-2592-7m3g-7fq6), DB error masking (GHSA-2vhw-q7vh-7xv2), refresh token security (GHSA-4rh7-jwg9-m28m), key bundle signature verification (GHSA-8h88-gxp3-j7pg), TOTP rate limiter backend (GHSA-h45m-mgcp-q388), .so path validation (GHSA-j48q-4c78-rhf9), password-file/fd support (GHSA-h3m5-p59h-x88p), jsonschema validation enforcement (GHSA-425g-fjhq-5h92). Bumped dependencies to resolve 8 additional Dependabot alerts. Feature-complete and security-audited, ready for release candidate testing.",
    "1.4.1rc1": "STREAMING ENCRYPTION & SECURITY HARDENING (RC1): Introduced Format Version 12 with streaming chunked AEAD encryption for constant-memory handling of large files. Added --info CLI action with encrypted_at timestamp metadata. Full Windows compatibility backport including NTFS ACL file permissions and automated Whirlpool build. Cross-platform tty_write/tty_clear_line helpers for terminal prompts that survive stdout/stderr redirection. All non-data output (progress, status, diagnostics) redirected to stderr via eprint(), enabling clean stdout capture for scripting. Security hardening: key zeroization across cascade/streaming/crypt_core, HKDF for keystore password wrap/streaming HMAC/pepper/PQC keys, per-layer salt and AAD for cascade (v12+), per-chunk nonce binding, chunk count validation against payload, plugin sandbox hardening (PL-4/5/6/H8/H9/H10), algorithm registries frozen after init, restrictive file permissions on keystore/pepper config (M1/M20), identity import fingerprint verification and path traversal validation (M5/H1/H2), PQCKeystore context manager for key zeroization, HSM pepper cache as bytearray. Performance: incremental metadata reads and bounded streaming decrypt avoid full file load. Dependency bumps: authlib 1.6.9, python-jose 3.4.0, cryptography ≥46.0.5.",
    "1.4.1rc2": "SECURITY DEPENDENCY UPDATE (RC2): Bumped nltk to 3.9.3 resolving CVE-2026-33236, CVE-2026-33231, CVE-2026-33230, GHSA-rf74-v2fm-23pw.",
    "1.4.2": "DESKTOP GUI & CRYPTO DEFAULTS: Added Simple/Pro mode toggle for desktop GUI — Simple mode (default) hides all advanced crypto options and uses the STANDARD template automatically. Modernized STANDARD template: hashes updated to SHA3-512 + BLAKE3 (10k rounds each), KDFs changed to RandomX (10 rounds) + Argon2 (10 rounds), encryption upgraded to 2-layer cascade (AES-256-GCM + ChaCha20-Poly1305). Independent XOR (v11) now enabled by default for STANDARD and PARANOID templates. Added RandomX support to independent XOR key derivation path. Added progress bars for multi-round Argon2 and RandomX in XOR mode. Dependency bumps: cryptography 46.0.6, requests 2.33.0, black 26.3.1, nltk 3.9.4.",
    "1.4.3": "BUGFIX: Fixed Flutter GUI launcher in Flatpak — corrected binary name in wrapper script so --gui flag works correctly. Fixed GTK window title from 'openssl_encrypt_mobile' to 'OpenSSL Encrypt'.",
    "1.4.4": "CLI TOOLING & HARDWARE TOKENS: 'info' action now reconstructs the full encrypt CLI from file metadata (cipher/cascade chain, all KDFs, hash-rounds flags, HSM and remote-pepper binding) with round-trip test coverage; JSON output unchanged. derive-password gains HSM-aware deterministic derivation (--hsm/--hsm-slot) and --confirm double-prompt. Diceware passphrase generation for generate-password (--dice with --dice-count/--dice-sep/--dice-list, bundled EFF large wordlist, entropy reporting, secrets.SystemRandom). New OnlyKey Challenge-Response HSM plugin with identity protection and cross-device deterministic pepper guarantee (YubiKey and OnlyKey provisioned with the same HMAC secret derive identical peppers). --hsm-slot no longer hard-restricted to slots 1-2 at the argparse layer (YubiKey validates 1-2, OnlyKey 1-12). No new Python dependencies; ~200 new unit tests. SECURITY HARDENING: Fixes for security-review findings, no on-disk format changes - all existing files remain decryptable. Authenticated v2 keystore format: HMAC-SHA256 over the whole keystore structure keyed by a domain-separated subkey of the master key, fails closed on tampering (key swaps, default repointing, entry deletion), legacy v1 keystores auto-upgrade on save (H4/M6). D-Bus per-caller authorization: session bus requires caller UID == service UID, system bus (explicit --system flag) enforces polkit CheckAuthorization with auth_admin_keep policies, fail-closed on missing sender/UID/polkit, mode-aware path policy (H7+L8). PQC algorithm resolution fails closed instead of silently downgrading to the weakest available KEM; legacy Kyber names still normalize at the same security level (H3). Portable USB drives derive master keys with a unique random per-drive salt instead of a global fixed salt; legacy drives still verify (H5). Plugin sandbox enforces file/network/process restrictions on the default process-isolation path, closes the AST frame/traceback escape chain and rejects world-writable plugin locations (H8). Balloon KDF gets a persisted memory-hard default space_cost on the v11 path (M3). Weak 10k-iteration PBKDF2 dual-encryption verifier no longer written to metadata (M7). Identity layer gains TOFU key-change detection with IdentityKeyChangedError and full-fingerprint lookup (M8). secure_memzero reports honestly on immutable input and the KDF password wipe is effective and copy-free across backends (M10/M10a). Metadata schema validation registers schemas dynamically and fails closed on unknown format_version, activating the previously unregistered v9/v12 schemas (M11). Also fixes a pytest collection regression that silently skipped 91 tests in unittests.py since 2025-12-30. FIXED: ML-KEM hybrid encryption via the CLI was impossible with any spelling - the ml_kem_patch compatibility shim converted ml-kem-*-hybrid to the legacy kyber*-hybrid names before argument handling and the v1.2.0 deprecation gate then rejected the converted name; the gate now judges the name the user actually typed (legacy kyber names remain blocked for new encryptions), and a latent missing pqc_gen_key attribute default on the subcommand path that this unmasked was fixed as well. Regression-tested through the real CLI entry point (test_mlkem_cli_regression.py). INTERNAL: repo-wide code-quality campaign (black/isort formatting, .flake8 config, autoflake unused-import cleanup with restoration of optional-dependency import probes, bare-except elimination) fixing four undefined-name (F821) bugs along the way, including a missing logger in keystore_cli.py and a missing eprint import in versions.py. CROSS-DEVICE HSM: yubikey_hsm/onlykey_hsm now form a protocol-compatible family - files encrypted with one device decrypt with the other via explicit --hsm when both hold the same HMAC-SHA1 secret; --hsm-slot on decrypt/rekey overrides the metadata slot (previously printed but silently ignored). OnlyKey plugin fixed for real hardware (PID masquerade as OTP-only YubiKey, OtpConnection request, actionable challenge-rejection errors) and new ONLYKEY_SETUP.md guide (udev rules, unlock-then-touch flow, challenge-code caveat). threefish_native builds on Python 3.14 (PyO3 0.26). Declared Python floor raised to >=3.11 to match the pinned dependency set (numpy 2.3), 3.14 classifiers added.",
    "1.4.5": "SECURITY DEPENDENCY UPDATE: urllib3 2.7.0 (CVE-2026-44431 proxied-redirect header forwarding, CVE-2026-44432 decompression-bomb bypass), cryptography 46.0.7 (CVE-2026-39892 non-contiguous buffer overflow), pillow 12.2.0 (PSD tile-extent integer-overflow OOB write), idna 3.15 (CVE-2024-3651 incomplete-fix follow-up); dev-only: authlib 1.6.12, pygments 2.20.0, pytest 9.0.3. All pins verified clean against OSV. INTERNAL: new flatpak-pin-check CI job verifies flatpak manifest pins against requirements-prod.txt on every push (caught a stale requests pin); manifest pins aligned.",
    "1.4.6": "PIV / PKCS#11 HSM BACKEND: New signature-based hardware backend (--hsm piv) that derives the key-derivation pepper from a PIV private key on a PKCS#11 token (YubiKey Bio MPE, Token2 PIN+ R3.3+, or any compliant PIV smart card), complementing the existing HMAC-SHA1 challenge-response backends (yubikey_hsm/onlykey_hsm). The key signs a deterministic challenge derived from the salt and the signature is normalized into a pepper via HKDF-SHA256, so the same key on multiple devices always yields identical peppers. Algorithm-agnostic across Ed25519 and RSA-2048/3072/4096; non-deterministic schemes (ECDSA, RSA-PSS) are explicitly rejected. New flags: --hsm-pkcs11-lib PATH (required), --hsm-piv-slot {9a,9c,9d,9e}, --hsm-biometric; --hsm-slot selects the PKCS#11 slot index for PIV. PIN is read via getpass (never via CLI args/logs/exceptions), held in a bytearray, and wiped with secure_memzero after login; a final-try guard refuses lock-risking attempts without confirmation; sessions are closed on every exit path. New dependency python-pkcs11 (requirements-hsm.txt). DOCS: PIV backend documented across README, hardware-tokens overview, the HSM plugin guide, the docs sidebar and VERSION.md (setup guide: docs/PIV_BACKEND.md). SOURCE-CODE INTEGRITY PROTECTION: New verify-integrity CLI command backed by a PGP-signed manifest of SHA-512 hashes over the core cryptographic/security source files (explicit allowlist in openssl_encrypt/integrity/protected_files.txt). Both source-scope and installed-scope manifests are maintained and shipped in the package, with a pre-commit hook that regenerates and drift-checks them. The bundled verifier is a convenience tripwire only — authoritative verification is manual with a trusted gpg against the out-of-band signing fingerprint D269D6A5D6D7CE52CE1FC71DC2DF29059ED65043 (also published in SECURITY.md). Signing key rotated from the bootstrap key to the production key. Runbook: docs/SOURCE_INTEGRITY.md. ASCII ARMOR: new encrypt --armor/-a flag writes PEM-style Base64 (ASCII-armored) output that is safe to paste into email, chat, YAML or a clipboard; the encrypted blob is wrapped in a BEGIN/END OPENSSL-ENCRYPT MESSAGE envelope with an OpenPGP-style CRC-24 checksum that detects paste truncation (fails closed on malformed armor). decrypt/info/verify auto-detect armored input by content (no flag needed) and the round-trip is byte-exact; works across the symmetric, stdin->stdout streaming and recipient/asymmetric output paths. New module modules/armor.py, covered by the signed integrity manifest. ENCRYPT-TO-SELF: when encrypting for recipients (--for-identity), the sender's own identity (--sign-with) is now added as an additional recipient by default so the sender can later decrypt their own outbound file (removing a common data-loss footgun); the sender slot reuses the existing multi-recipient ML-KEM wrapping and is de-duplicated by fingerprint. Opt out with --no-encrypt-to-self. DETACHED FILE SIGNING: new sign/verify-signature actions produce and verify a detached post-quantum (ML-DSA-65) signature over an arbitrary file, closing the authenticity gap of symmetric AEAD (where anyone who knows the password can forge a valid file). sign --input F --sign-with IDENTITY writes a .sig JSON sidecar (ASCII-armored by default, --no-armor for raw) binding the file's SHA-512 to the signer over a domain-separated payload distinct from the encrypted-file metadata signature. verify-signature resolves the signer's public key from the local identity store and contacts by the embedded fingerprint, fails closed on an unknown signer (--signer to pin), reports which component(s) verified, and supports --json. The sidecar signatures list is forward-compatible with a future classical hybrid component. New module modules/file_signature.py, covered by the signed integrity manifest. ENVELOPE ENCRYPTION (DEK/KEK): new opt-in --envelope mode encrypts bulk data under a random Data Encryption Key (DEK) and wraps that DEK with a Key Encryption Key derived from the password through the full unchanged KDF chain (stored as encryption.wrapped_dek; decryption auto-detects). Enables O(header) credential rekey - changing the password rewraps the small DEK and rewrites only the metadata header, retaining the bulk ciphertext verbatim instead of re-encrypting it (this rotates the access credential, not the data key; full re-encrypt remains available for true data-key rotation). The bulk AEAD binds a canonical stable subset of the metadata that excludes only the KEK-gating fields a rekey changes (KDF salt/config + wrapped_dek), so a rekey keeps the ciphertext valid while tampering any authenticated field still fails closed. For a cascade chain the DEK is wrapped under the SAME chain (not single AES-256-GCM) so the envelope is never weaker than the payload. Opt-in and fully backward-compatible: non-envelope files are byte-for-byte unchanged; DEK/KEK are zeroed on every path and never logged. Ported from the 1.5.x line with an interoperable wrapped-DEK format for non-XChaCha cascade chains. New module modules/envelope.py. SEQUENTIAL XOR LAST-STAGE CANCELLATION FIX (SECURITY, ADVISORY 2026-02, CWE-916): in sequential XOR composition (--xor, format_version 8/10) the chain's final value was XOR'd into the key in addition to the last stage's own snapshot — being equal, the two cancelled and the last stage dropped out of the key. For a single-KDF/no-prior-hash configuration (e.g. Argon2-only) the key collapsed to the cheap initial SHA256(password+salt) computed before the chain, bypassing the configured KDF cost; with the default multi-KDF + chained-hash configuration only the last stage cancels, so the cost was at most marginally reduced (NOT bypassed) — neither precondition is the default. Fixed at the new format_version 13 (xor_mode=sequential), which omits the redundant append so every stage contributes; --xor now writes v13 and decrypt routes purely by the on-disk xor_mode field. Legacy v8/v10 files still decrypt unchanged (append-only) but remain weak until re-encrypted. FORMAT VERSION 13 — INDEPENDENT XOR DEFAULT: independent XOR now derives a distinct HKDF-SHA256(salt_0, info=\"openssl_encrypt.indep-xor.v13.salt:\"+name) salt per component instead of feeding every component the same salt_0, removing a cancellation footgun where two identical components could XOR to zero; v13 is now the default for --independent-xor, the STANDARD/PARANOID templates and rekey, is byte-identical across the 1.4.x and 1.5.x lines (pinned by a cross-line golden vector), with v11 still fully supported for decryption. STREAMING DECRYPT FIX (Issue #50): full-pipeline streaming encrypt->decrypt failed because the password key was derived at the original format_version before the streaming decision pinned format_version 12 (so encrypt and decrypt keys diverged and every chunk failed authentication with InvalidTag); the streaming path now decides streaming and pins v12 BEFORE key derivation, matching decryption, with new full-pipeline regression coverage (TestFullPipelineStreamingRoundtrip).",
    "1.4.7": "DOCUMENTATION / PACKAGING RELEASE: refresh README.md (rendered as the PyPI project page) for the 1.4.6 feature set. Updated the What's New and release-history sections, and corrected the KDF Composition Modes section, which still described Independent XOR v11 as the STANDARD/PARANOID default and pointed at a now-superseded re-injection TODO; it now documents format version 13 (per-component domain-separated HKDF salts) as the default and records the sequential-XOR last-stage cancellation fix (ADVISORY 2026-02) as shipped. No functional, CLI, or on-disk format changes; cut solely to refresh the immutable PyPI project page, which still showed 1.4.5-era content after the 1.4.6 upload.",
    "1.4.8": "SECURITY REVIEW FOLLOW-UP & PASSWORD-STRENGTH TOOLING. CHECK-PASSWORD COMMAND: new read-only check-password subcommand reports password strength without encrypting anything — pattern-aware category, pattern-aware and raw entropy, detected-weakness warnings, and pass/fail against --password-policy (with --strict-strength); reads the password from CRYPT_PASSWORD, piped stdin, an interactive prompt, or (discouraged, with a warning) -p/--password; human output to stderr, --json machine-readable report to stdout; exits non-zero when a policy fails so it works as a scriptable gate (backported from 1.5.x). PATTERN-AWARE PASSWORD STRENGTH: strength feedback now detects predictable structure (dictionary words, l33t substitutions, keyboard walks, ascending/descending sequences, repeats, dates) instead of raw character-space entropy alone (which over-rated structured passwords like Password1!); uses the optional zxcvbn package when installed with a built-in heuristic fallback otherwise (zxcvbn is NOT a production dependency); advisory by default (the displayed label and new Password-weakness warnings reflect the pattern-aware estimate, but the --min-password-entropy gate still uses the raw measure so previously-accepted passwords remain accepted); the new --strict-strength flag (auto-enabled by the paranoid policy) makes the gate reject passwords whose pattern-aware strength is too low; affects encryption-time validation only, never decryption (backported from 1.5.x). DEBUG WARNING PROPORTIONALITY: the loud SENSITIVE DATA LOGGING ACTIVE banner now fires only for --debug --unsafe-show-secrets (the path that prints cleartext); plain --debug, which redacts every secret to length plus a keyed SHA-256 fingerprint, now shows a calm accurate note; message-only change, no redaction logic altered. SECURITY REVIEW FOLLOW-UP (2026-07-07, each fixed with a regression test and crypto-reviewer sign-off): H1 [HSM-1] the hsm fido2-test command printed the full 32-byte derived hardware pepper as hex to stdout unconditionally, outside the debug_secret() redaction chokepoint — the hex dump is removed (length still reported). M1 [PLUGIN-2] the plugin loader verified a signature over one read while AST-scanning and executing others (verify-A/execute-B) and could exec a cached .pyc never covered by the signature — _validate_plugin_file now reads the plugin once as raw bytes threaded through the signature gate, the sha256(raw) pin and ast.parse(raw), and load_plugin re-reads once, compares to the pin, and executes via compile(raw)+exec instead of spec.loader.exec_module, killing the CRLF/BOM and .pyc-shadow discrepancies (fail-closed on a missing pin for non-built-ins). H2 [PLUGIN-1] a package plugin signed only its __init__.py while transitively importing unverified sibling modules (a signed __init__.py plus a malicious unsigned helper.py executed unchecked) — a signed per-package PLUGIN.manifest with one detached PLUGIN.manifest.asc now covers every importable module (source .py, bytecode .pyc, native .so/.pyd, recursively including underscore-prefixed and nested), verified via the existing trust anchors plus an exact on-disk tree match with every module hash-pinned and every source sibling AST-scanned; under enforce a tampered/unlisted/native-swapped/impostor-signed sibling is refused, warn warns and loads, built-in packages keep the trust shortcut; plugin sign auto-detects a package and writes the manifest (a runtime import-hook for the validation-to-import TOCTOU window remains a documented follow-on). M2 [MEM-1] several callers wrapped an immutable bytes secret in bytearray(...) and called secure_memzero on the throwaway copy (a no-op wipe) in parallel_kdf.py, asymmetric_core.py, pqc.py and crypt_core.py — each secret is now held in a bytearray/SecureBytes from creation and that object is wiped. M3 [KDF-1] the parallel and sequential Independent-XOR paths derived different keys for Argon2 rounds greater than 1 — the parallel worker now matches the sequential derivation exactly. R1 [KDF-2b] a RandomX failure in the parallel Independent-XOR path was swallowed (fail-open) unlike the sequential path — both now fail closed. M4 [STREAM-1] a streaming per-chunk AEAD authentication failure was classified by substring-matching the exception text — classification is now structural. KDF DEBUG-LEAK AND V8/V10 REFUSAL (audit 2026-07-06): per-round Argon2/Balloon/Scrypt debug lines printed round_salt as raw hex, which for format_version 7+ rounds 1+ is the first 128 bits of the live derived-key chain — now redacted by default (cleartext only under --unsafe-show-secrets); and new files are no longer written in the cost-bypassing v8/v10 sequential-XOR format (which appended the chain final value twice, cancelling the last KDF stage so an Argon2-only config collapsed to about SHA256(password+salt)): encrypt_file now defaults to format_version 9 and refuses to encrypt new v8/v10 files (existing v8/v10 files still decrypt; a library-only allow_insecure_legacy_xor escape hatch remains for legacy fixtures), and rekey transparently upgrades an inherited v8/v10 file to v9 including the envelope fast-path. STRING_ENTROPY UNICODE FIX: non-ASCII characters (accented letters, other scripts, emoji) were excluded from the unique-character count and could score a password at 0.0 bits; they now contribute (ASCII scores unchanged) and a misleading constant-time docstring claim was corrected (backported from 1.5.x). CROSS-LINE PQC KEM COMPATIBILITY (finding #83 backport, 2026-07-10): PQC KEM symmetric keys for format_version 12+ files are now derived with HKDF-SHA256 (algorithm-name domain separation) matching the 1.5.x line, so v12 (streaming) and v13 (Independent-XOR) PQC files finally interoperate across the two maintenance lines; files written by 1.4.x releases up to 1.4.7 with the legacy bare-SHA256 derivation remain fully decryptable via an authenticated one-shot legacy retry (the AEAD tag rejects wrong keys, so no downgrade surface) that prints a re-encryption recommendation; sub-v12 and non-PQC files derive byte-identically to before. EMBEDDED PQC KEY FIX: v11-v13 files with an embedded password-encrypted post-quantum private key always failed to decrypt (Missing PQC key salt) because the salt lookup was gated on format versions 4-10 — the gate now covers every v4+ format, with regression round-trips. FORMAT VERSION 14 GROUNDWORK (not yet the default): new independent-XOR-only format version 14 with a length-prefixed TLV KDF seed — password, salt and hardware pepper enter the key derivation as unambiguous length-prefixed fields instead of raw concatenation (finding #100 boundary ambiguity) — plus fail-closed guards (no v14 sequential-XOR file can exist, unknown future versions are refused even without the schema validator) and cross-line-pinned golden vectors; all files below v14 derive byte-identically to before; additionally v14 PQC files bind the KEM symmetric key to the full transcript (algorithm, AEAD choice and a digest of the KEM encapsulation ciphertext) via HKDF-SHA256, with the legacy decrypt retry scoped to v12/v13 only. FORMAT VERSION 14 IS NOW THE DEFAULT WRITE FORMAT: new encryptions default to the independent-XOR robust-combiner topology (stays as strong as its strongest KDF component instead of the sequential cascade's weakest-link floor), streaming writes v14 (streaming PQC gets the transcript binding), rekey with --independent-xor targets v14, and wide keys (AES-SIV, Threefish-512/1024) derive at full length on every v14 path (M1); sequential XOR remains a supported opt-in pinned at format version 13, explicit version requests are honored, and every existing file decrypts unchanged. STREAMING + SEQUENTIAL-XOR DATA-LOSS FIX: that combination silently wrote files that always failed chunk authentication on decrypt; it is now refused with a clear error pointing to --no-streaming. KEYSTORE VERSION-GATE FIX: keystore dual-encryption metadata lookups were gated on format versions 4-10, so v11-v13 files (including the 1.4.6/1.4.7 template default) failed keystore dual-encryption — the gates now cover every v4+ format. RANDOMX WIDE-KEY FIX: RandomX-enabled configs crashed key derivation for AES-SIV/Threefish (the 32-byte RandomX output is now HKDF-normalized to the full key length; 32-byte-key files are unaffected). V14 REVIEW REMEDIATIONS (crypto-reviewer pass 2026-07-10, two Low findings): the v14 TLV KDF seed is hashed through a memoryview instead of an immutable bytes copy, so no unwipeable copy of the cleartext password/salt/pepper is materialized (M2 [MEM-1] hygiene; derived keys byte-identical per the cross-line golden vectors), and the KEM transcript-binding documentation now states that ciphertext/metadata substitution is detected via AEAD authentication (wrong key fails the tag), so the PQC symmetric layer must remain an AEAD (all reachable PQC data ciphers are AEADs). V14 SEED ENCODER SINGLE-ALLOCATION (post-v14 review LOW-1, 2026-07-11): the v14 TLV KDF seed was built with incremental bytearray growth, so CPython reallocations could leave partial copies of the length-prefixed cleartext password in freed unwiped heap (the final secure_memzero wipes only the last allocation) and the bytes() field conversion materialized an unwipeable copy of mutable secrets — the seed is now written into one exact-size preallocated bytearray through memoryviews (no reallocation, no immutable copies; M2 [MEM-1] hygiene); not exploitable on its own, derived keys and the on-disk format are byte-identical (cross-line golden vectors plus a new byte-identity test matrix). KEYSTORE VERSION-GATE TYPE SAFETY (post-v14 review LOW-2, 2026-07-11): the keystore integration read format_version from raw parsed JSON and compared it with >=, so a crafted file carrying a non-int value crashed the operation with an unhandled TypeError (fail-closed either way — no bypass, key material never touched, but a crash is a one-operation DoS) — all six affected ingestion points in keystore_wrapper/keystore_utils now validate the type once via a shared helper and reject malformed metadata with a clean ValidationError; legitimately written files always store an int, so behavior for every valid file is unchanged. RECIPIENT PASSWORD-WRAP TRANSCRIPT BINDING (post-v14 review LOW-3, 2026-07-11): the per-recipient AES-256-GCM wrap key was derived from the ML-KEM shared secret with a static HKDF info (the finding-#83 transcript binding covered only the main PQC data path) — new asymmetric files now bind the KEM encapsulation ciphertext (SHA-256 digest) and the KEM algorithm identity into the wrap-key derivation and record wrap_version 3 in the signed recipient entry; defense-in-depth (ML-KEM implicit rejection plus the GCM tag already defeat substitution in practice), fail-closed by construction (a marked entry never falls back to weaker derivations, a stripped marker derives the wrong key and fails the tag, unknown markers are rejected); every existing file keeps decrypting via the unchanged v2->v1 chain (pinned by a pre-fix fixture), while asymmetric files written from this version on cannot be opened by older releases (clean unwrap error — same trade as the v14 default flip). REMOTE/COMBINED PEPPER ZEROIZATION (v14 follow-up review LOW-1, gitlab#113, 2026-07-11): the remote pepper (AES-GCM decrypt output) and the combined hsm+remote pepper concatenation were immutable bytes that outlived the carefully wiped v14 TLV KDF seed, and the hsm_pepper wipe was a no-op copy-zero on plugin-supplied bytes (M10) — all three are now held in wipeable bytearrays from creation (the combined pepper built by a new _combine_peppers helper in one exact-size allocation, M2 [MEM-1] standard) and zeroized in the encrypt/decrypt finally blocks on all paths; unavoidable immutable library/plugin transients remain documented accepted residuals; no derived keys or file formats change. FUTURE FORMAT-VERSION WRITE BOUND (v14 follow-up review LOW-2, gitlab#114, 2026-07-11): encrypt_file accepted an explicit format_version above the latest stable format and wrote a file the decrypt side fails closed on (permanently unreadable — data-loss footgun for API callers, not attacker-exploitable); new writes now raise a clear ValueError for format_version > LATEST_STABLE_FORMAT_VERSION before any output is produced, mirroring the decrypt-side bound; no change for any valid version. LEGACY-KDF RETRY CLASSIFICATION (v14 follow-up review LOW-4, gitlab#116, 2026-07-11): two fail-closed classification gaps fixed — the adapter retry now also catches the project AuthenticationError raised by the custom XChaCha wrapper (adapter xchacha legacy files previously skipped the retry), and the native classifier is narrowed from except-Exception to (InvalidTag, AuthenticationError) so structural errors propagate instead of triggering a pointless retry (the 1.5.x threefish part does not apply on this line); the retry stays scoped to v12/v13 and AEAD-gated, v14+ never retries. HSM TEST-COMMAND PEPPER PRINT — LIVE-PATH RESIDUAL (H1 [HSM-1] residual, gitlab#121, 2026-07-12): the earlier H1 fix removed the pepper hex dump from modules/hsm_cli.py, a click frontend that is not wired into the openssl-encrypt entry point — the live hsm fido2-test/onlykey-test handlers in crypt_cli.py still printed the full derived hardware pepper as hex; both now report only the length (mitigating: the printed pepper came from a random per-invocation test salt, so it exposed no real file, but it is hardware-derived key material); additionally the FIDO2 pepper plugin no longer interpolates raw prf/hmac-secret output (the pepper's source material) into debug logs or PluginResult error messages that reach the user via eprint (structure only: type/keys), and the asymmetric_core __main__ self-test prints lengths instead of its (random, throwaway) roundtrip password hex on failure; the H1 regression test now scans the live crypt_cli.py, the OnlyKey/YubiKey challenge-response plugins and every prf_data sink, not just hsm_cli.py.",
    "1.4.9": "SECURITY HARDENING (in development): DERIVE-PASSWORD MEMORY HYGIENE (security review 2026-07-13 LOW-1, gitlab#123): the derive-password handler held the HSM pepper as unwipeable immutable bytes and wiped only a throwaway copy of the derived key — the pepper is now held in a wipeable buffer from acquisition (a mutable plugin buffer is wiped in place) and the truncated output buffer is a bytearray, both zeroized in a finally on all exit paths; the immutable generate_key return remains a documented accepted residual (M10); derived outputs unchanged. LEGACY SEED ZEROIZATION (security review 2026-07-13 INFO-1, gitlab#124): the no-hash-iterations branch of generate_key built its KDF seed as an immutable password+salt+pepper concatenation the return-site wipe silently refused (M10) — the seed is now written into one exact-size wipeable bytearray through a memoryview and effectively zeroized in the finally, including when a KDF rebinds the working variable; legacy sequential formats only, derived keys byte-identical (golden-value regression tests). BALLOON V14 FAIL-CLOSED (security review 2026-07-13 INFO-2, gitlab#125): the decrypt-side balloon derivation fell back to the weak space_cost=16 when the field was absent from metadata — kept for v11-13 where it is load-bearing (pre-M3 v1.4.0-v1.4.3 files really derived with 16), but refused with a clear ValueError for format_version >= 14, where every released writer persists the field and a missing value can only be crafted/corrupted metadata; the parallel path needs no gate (v13+ parallel dispatch delegates to the sequential gated path, invariant pinned by a test); no legitimately written file changes behavior. KDF COST MEMORY CEILING (gitlab#128, GHSA-7894-5gw8-69hr): Argon2 memory_cost, scrypt N and balloon space_cost read from untrusted file metadata or a keystore header were fed to the KDF before authentication with no upper bound (scrypt's own maxmem was even derived from the attacker's N, so it could never trip), letting a single crafted file drive a multi-terabyte allocation and OOM-crash the host on decrypt with no correct password — decryption now estimates peak memory before any KDF runs and refuses above an 8 GiB safety ceiling (4x the largest shipped preset, so no legitimate file is affected), overridable per file with --allow-high-kdf-cost or an interactive confirmation; the keystore load path is guarded identically and the scrypt memory estimate (previously under-reported as zero) is corrected; CPU/time cost stays advisory since only memory OOM-kills uninterruptibly. IDENTITY-FILE KDF COST BOUND (gitlab#129, GHSA-783h-8q2f-f762): an identity file's password-protection block carried an unbounded Argon2 memory_cost read straight from JSON that _derive_key fed to hash_secret_raw before the AEAD tag authenticated the private key, so a tampered/attacker-authored identity with a gigabyte-scale memory_cost OOM-crashed the host on unlock pre-authentication (same class as gitlab#128 on the identity-file surface) — the Argon2 cost parameters are now clamped to sane maxima (memory <= 2 GiB, time <= 64, parallelism <= 16) before derivation; legitimate identities use the 64 MB default, far under the cap, so no file changes behavior. PLUGIN SIGNATURE ENFORCE-BY-DEFAULT (gitlab#130, GHSA-587j-4r3v-cm2c): the plugin loader defaulted to the warn signature policy, so an unsigned/unverifiable non-built-in plugin was exec'd in the host process after only a bypassable AST denylist scan (getattr/__import__ indirection), giving arbitrary code execution to anyone able to drop a .py into a plugin directory — the default policy is now enforce: a non-built-in plugin must carry a valid detached signature from an enrolled trust anchor or it is refused before its code is imported; built-in bundled plugins keep their trust shortcut and are unaffected, so no shipped functionality changes; users who deliberately load unsigned third-party plugins can opt back in with OPENSSL_ENCRYPT_PLUGIN_SIGNATURE_POLICY=warn (or off) or an explicit signature_policy= argument, and an unrecognized env value now fails closed to enforce with a warning instead of silently weakening the policy; the built-in trust shortcut is now scoped to the genuinely shipped plugin subtree so the advertised third-party drop directories (plugins/user, plugins/community, plugins/official) are no longer treated as built-in and must pass the full signature+AST+TOCTOU gate (otherwise the ENFORCE default would have been bypassable by dropping an unsigned plugin there). PQC KEYFILE ARGON2ID WRAP (gitlab#131 F16, GHSA-fmjx-p826-6fvr): a .pqc keyfile created with --pqc-keyfile wrapped the long-lived PQC private key under a PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 key at only 100,000 iterations (below the OWASP floor, far weaker than the Argon2id used for file/keystore material), so an attacker who obtained the keyfile could brute-force the wrapping password cheaply offline — new keyfiles now derive the wrapping key with Argon2id and record a self-describing key_kdf descriptor (redundant trailing SHA-256 dropped); existing PBKDF2-wrapped keyfiles still decrypt unchanged via the legacy no-key_kdf path, and the Argon2 cost read from a keyfile is bounded (memory <= 2 GiB, time <= 64, parallelism <= 16) so a tampered keyfile cannot OOM the host on decrypt before the AES-GCM tag authenticates; re-wrap existing keyfiles to move them onto Argon2id. DUAL-ENCRYPTION WEAK VERIFIER REMOVED (gitlab#131 F18, GHSA-fmjx-p826-6fvr): dual-encrypted files carried a pqc_dual_encrypt_verify hash (a 10k-iteration PBKDF2 pre-check over the file password stored in cleartext metadata) that an attacker with the file could brute-force offline to recover the second-factor file password — that pre-check is no longer recomputed, trusted, or propagated into re-processed metadata; the file password is authenticated by the dual-encryption AES-GCM tag during keystore key retrieval (keystore Argon2id KDF, not brute-forceable offline), and the one state where that tag would not gate the file password (a file claiming dual encryption backed by a non-dual keystore entry — a metadata/keystore mismatch) now fails closed in get_key; legacy dual-encrypted files still decrypt unchanged, re-encrypt them to drop the weak verifier from metadata. PORTABLE-USB INTEGRITY + MANIFEST SALT (gitlab#132 F13/F19, GHSA-8jx3-27qf-3p97): the portable-USB threat model treats the removable drive as untrusted, but integrity verification only re-hashed the files listed in the manifest — a file ADDED to the drive, including a root-level autorun.* payload (auto-executed by the OS on insert), was never detected and verification still passed; new drives now write a v2 integrity manifest that is an allowlist of every file in the tool tree (plus the root autorun hashes), so verification flags ANY file added afterward — a planted .dll/.so/.pyd/.exe or any other payload, not just a fixed extension set — and any tampered/added/removed root autorun file (the user's mutable workspace data/ and logs/ are excluded, so normal use still verifies); drives created before the fix carry no v2 marker and verify exactly as before, re-create a drive to gain the stronger checks; additionally the cryptographic hash-manifest fallback path now derives its key with the drive's unique per-drive salt instead of the global fixed salt (F19). LOW SECURITY BATCH (gitlab#133-136): PLUGIN SANDBOX PATH PREFIX (F15, GHSA-vr4h-5xqv-xxxf) — PluginSandbox._is_safe_path authorized paths with a bare string prefix, so a sandboxed plugin foo could access a sibling .../plugins/foobar; each allowed directory is now matched as itself or with a trailing separator, refusing prefix-sharing siblings. KEYSERVER TOKEN DEBUG REDACTION (F17, GHSA-jqqp-pf9j-889j) — the 'keyserver set-token <token>' positional bearer token was echoed cleartext in the --debug argv dump; it is now routed through the debug_secret redaction chokepoint (rotate any token previously used with --debug). TRUST-ANCHOR FULL-FINGERPRINT (F21, GHSA-xg52-638v-jc5m) — enroll_trust_key accepted a suffix/partial fingerprint match, so a forgeable short key id could enrol an attacker's colliding key as a trusted plugin-signing anchor; enrollment now requires the exact full fingerprint. PIV PIN MEMORY HYGIENE (F20) — the PIN is now decoded from its wiped bytearray directly instead of through an extra immutable bytes() copy, materializing one fewer unwipeable copy (the binding-required str is an accepted minimized-lifetime residual); no behavior change. DESKTOP GUI ASYMMETRIC DECRYPTION (gitlab#137, github#55): the Flutter desktop GUI Decrypt tab now exposes controls to select a decryption identity and control sender-signature verification (--with-key/--verify-from/--no-verify), previously wired into the GUI CLI bridge but unreachable from any widget; skipping signature verification is off by default behind an explicit warned opt-in, and identity lists are de-duplicated (empty names skipped) so a duplicate or blank identity name cannot crash the tab. DESKTOP GUI PASSWORD GENERATOR (gitlab#139, github#57): the desktop GUI gains a Password Generator screen that produces passwords via the CLI generate-password --json command in both character mode (length plus lowercase/uppercase/digits/special toggles) and diceware passphrase mode (word count, separator, optional custom wordlist), showing the entropy/strength and offering copy-to-clipboard. DESKTOP GUI SECURE SHRED (gitlab#140, github#58): the desktop GUI gains a Secure Shred screen that securely overwrites and deletes files/directories via the CLI shred command with a selectable pass count and recursive toggle, gated behind a mandatory irreversible-action confirmation dialog."

}

# Check for updates
def get_latest_version():
    """Return the latest version number."""
    return __version__

def get_version_info():
    """Return version information as a dictionary."""
    return {
        "version": __version__,
        "git_commit": __git_commit__,
        "author": __author__,
        "license": __license__,
        "copyright": __copyright__,
        "history": VERSION_HISTORY
    }

def print_version_info():
    """Print version information to the console."""
    print(f"Secure File Encryption Tool v{__version__}")
    print(f"Git commit: {__git_commit__}")
    print(f"Author: {__author__}")
    print(f"License: {__license__}")
    print(f"{__copyright__}")
    print("\nVersion History:")
    for version, description in VERSION_HISTORY.items():
        print(f"  {version}: {description}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    print_version_info()
